### What do you want to change?

I wanted a new extension event - "user_bash_complete" or something similar that gets emitted when an bang-ed command submitted by user is completed (success, error or cancelled)

### Why?

I wanted to create an extension that would auto-call the next turn of the agent loop once the user executed shell command is completed. 
Here is the extension source code - https://github.com/stagefright5/pi-agent-extensions/blob/main/extensions/bang-dont-ghost/README.md

Disclosure: The **extension** is generated by an LLM running in the pi cli, but **NOT** this issue

I was reviewing the code and it felt pretty complex for a simple extension.

Basically,
* there is poller that gets attached when `"user_bash"` event is emitted the command gets stored in a pending array and it keeps on polling for the next session entry by waiting on the session_entries_length+1 item.
* The polling continues till there is an entry that matches the pending entry (eg. "bashExecution echo hi" matches the "echo hi" entry which would be in the "pending" entries array).
* Also, as soon as a match is found, an empty custom message is created and sent to the LLM (withing the pi's agent loop)
* This tracking + polling happens whenever there is "user_bash" event, and till the "pending" entries in the tracker is empty

NOTE: LLM explaination with ascii diagrams - https://github.com/stagefright5/pi-agent-extensions/blob/main/extensions/bang-dont-ghost/WHY_POLLING_EXISTS.md

All of this could have been collapsed into this if there was a "user_bash_complete" event that an extension can listen on:

 ```ts
   pi.on("user_bash_complete", (event) => {
      if (!event.excludeFromContext && !event.result.cancelled) {
         pi.sendMessage(emptyHiddenMessage, {
            triggerTurn: true,
            deliverAs: "followUp",
         });
      }
   });
 ```

### How? (optional)

- Emit an even whenever the "user_bash" message is complete
- At the end of `recordBashResult` in `agent_session.ts` https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/a69bef789bc95abf0acee16f7b4660b70b650bb9/packages/coding-agent/src/core/agent-session.ts#L2960, emit the `user_bash_complete` with this argument:
```
{
    type: "user_bash_result",
    command: bashMessage.command,
    excludeFromContext: bashMessage.excludeFromContext ?? false,
    result: {
        output: bashMessage.output,
        exitCode: bashMessage.exitCode,
        cancelled: bashMessage.cancelled,
        truncated: bashMessage.truncated,
        fullOutputPath: bashMessage.fullOutputPath,
    },
}
```
- An extension can listen to it like so:
 ```ts
   pi.on("user_bash_complete", (event) => {
      if (!event.excludeFromContext && !event.result.cancelled) {
         pi.sendMessage(emptyHiddenMessage, {
            triggerTurn: true,
            deliverAs: "followUp",
         });
      }
   });
 ```

### What happened?

`iterateSseMessages()` (`packages/ai/src/api/anthropic-messages.ts:763`) and `readPiMessagesEvents()` (`packages/ai/src/api/pi-messages.ts:327`) only release the reader lock when they stop:

```ts
finally {
	reader.releaseLock();
}
```

Releasing a lock does not cancel the stream. If parsing stops before the body ends — an Anthropic `event: error`, invalid JSON, or Pi Messages returning on its `done`/`error` event — the HTTP response body stays open until the server closes it. `parseSSE()` in `openai-codex-responses.ts` and `readMistralEvents()` in `mistral-conversations.ts` already call `reader.cancel()` before `releaseLock()`.

The Pi Messages path is the easiest to trigger: the caller returns as soon as it sees `done` or `error` (`pi-messages.ts:439`), which closes the generator while the SSE response can still be open.

### Steps to reproduce

Use a `ReadableStream` whose underlying source records `cancel()`, then:

- Anthropic: emit `event: error` with a data line and keep the stream open. The parser throws; `cancel` is never called.
- Pi Messages: emit a terminal `done` event and keep the stream open. The caller returns; `cancel` is never called.

The Codex stream test already uses this fixture (`packages/ai/test/openai-codex-stream.test.ts:499,554,600`) and asserts `cancelled === true`.

### Expected behavior

Cancel the body before releasing the reader, matching Codex/Mistral:

```ts
finally {
	await reader.cancel().catch(() => {});
	reader.releaseLock();
}
```

Version: 1.5.2 (`a9878e1`)

### What happened?

`consumeSseStream()` in `packages/mcp/src/transports/streamable-http.ts` keeps the pending event in one string:

```ts
buffered += text;
...
buffered = buffered.slice(start);
```

When a large event arrives as many chunks without a line boundary, every chunk appends to that string and `consumeLines()` rescans the whole accumulated prefix. This is the same quadratic pattern that #11761 fixed for the stdio line reader.

Measured on `a9878e1` (1.5.2), a single `data:` line delivered in 64 KiB chunks:

| event size | time |
| --- | --- |
| 1 MiB | 24.4 ms |
| 4 MiB | 288.6 ms |
| 8 MiB | 1107 ms |
| 16 MiB | 4507 ms |

`JsonRpcLineReader` handles a 16 MiB incomplete message in 4.9 ms.

`DEFAULT_MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES` is 16 MiB, so a large MCP tool result can block the event loop for several seconds. 4 MiB already takes ~290 ms.

### Steps to reproduce

Node 24, repo root, `--experimental-strip-types`. Feed `consumeSseStream` a stream that writes `data: `, then N bytes in 64 KiB chunks, then `\n\n`:

```ts
import { consumeSseStream } from "./packages/mcp/src/transports/streamable-http.ts";

const total = 16 * 1024 * 1024;
const chunk = new Uint8Array(64 * 1024).fill(0x61);
let sent = 0;
const stream = new ReadableStream({
	pull(controller) {
		if (sent === 0) { controller.enqueue(new TextEncoder().encode("data: ")); sent = 6; return; }
		if (sent < total) { controller.enqueue(chunk); sent += chunk.length; return; }
		controller.enqueue(new TextEncoder().encode("\n\n"));
		controller.close();
	},
});

const t0 = performance.now();
await consumeSseStream(stream, { maxEventBytes: total + 1024, onEvent: () => {} });
console.log(performance.now() - t0);
```

Vary `total` over 1/4/8/16 MiB; the time grows quadratically.

### Expected behavior

Parsing should be linear in event size. Suggested fix: keep the incomplete event as a chunk queue and scan only new bytes, joining once at the line boundary — the approach `JsonRpcLineReader` already uses. `readBodyText()` (`text += decoder.decode(...)`) has the same pattern for large JSON responses.

I can submit a fix if this is welcome.

Version: 1.5.2 (`a9878e1`)

## Summary

On 2026.9.4, a plugin that defers an embedded agent run from inside a hook callback (a completely ordinary `Promise`/timer chain) gets the run spuriously rejected with `GatewayDrainingError: Gateway is draining; new tasks are not accepted` — even though no gateway restart or host suspension is in progress. The deferred async work inherits the triggering command's **already-released root-work context** via `AsyncLocalStorage`, and the subordinate work-admission check judges the run by that released context.

If the same work is instead awaited synchronously inside the hook, the run is admitted but starves together with the draining `/new` lifecycle and always dies at exactly its configured timeout (observed with both 20s and 90s — while the model itself returns HTTP 200 headers in ~4s).

## Environment

- OpenClaw 2026.9.4 (npm, macOS arm64)
- Plugin: memory-lancedb-pro (third-party), but the mechanism is plugin-generic

## Observed behavior (production logs)

Synchronous hook (`await runEmbeddedAgent(...)` inside `command:new`):

```
memory-reflection: reflection generation start; timeoutMs=90000
[agent/embedded] embedded run timeout: timeoutMs=90000        ← always exactly at the cap
```

Deferred (`Promise.resolve().then(() => runEmbeddedAgent(...))`):

```
hook scheduled async reflection
sessions.create 62ms                                           ← lifecycle completes
embedded run rejected ~1.5s later:
GatewayDrainingError: Gateway is draining; new tasks are not accepted
```

`gateway-restart.log` confirms **no restart** at that time.

## Root cause (dist source, 2026.9.4)

1. `command-queue-CwgsqkaH.mjs:594` — `if (isGatewaySubordinateWorkAdmissionClosed()) return Promise.reject(new GatewayDrainingError())` (also `session-lifecycle-admission-*.mjs:547`)
2. `gateway-work-admission-DaOR_dL4.mjs` — `isGatewaySubordinateWorkAdmissionClosed()` reads `GATEWAY_WORK_ADMISSION_STATE.currentRootWork.getStore()` (AsyncLocalStorage) and returns `current.released` when a root work context is present
3. Hook callbacks execute inside the triggering command's root-work context. When `/new` completes, that root work is released. Any async resource created inside the hook (promises, timers) inherits the released store via ALS, so work submitted later is judged against a dead context and rejected.

Notably, the same module already exports `runWithGatewayIndependentRootWorkAdmission` ("Keeps the caller's async-resource owner") and `runWithGatewayDetachedWorkAdmission` ("Delayed producers outlive their triggering request and own their async cleanup") — the pattern is recognized internally, but the **plugin-facing `api.runtime.agent.runEmbeddedAgent` entry does not re-root or isolate the inherited context**, so plugin authors cannot safely defer embedded runs.

## Minimal reproduction

```js
// tiny plugin manifest: activation.onStartup
export function register(api) {
  api.registerHook("command:new", () => {
    // Defer like any normal fire-and-forget follow-up
    Promise.resolve().then(async () => {
      try {
        await api.runtime.agent.runEmbeddedAgent({
          sessionId: `repro-${Date.now()}`,
          sessionKey: "temp:repro",
          agentId: "main",
          sessionPersistence: "detached",
          prompt: "say hi",
          disableTools: true,
          timeoutMs: 15000,
        });
        api.logger.info("repro: run completed");
      } catch (err) {
        api.logger.warn(`repro: ${err.name}: ${err.message}`);
        // → repro: GatewayDrainingError: Gateway is draining; new tasks are not accepted
      }
    });
  });
}
```

Trigger `/new` in any session. The deferred run is rejected ~instantly.

## Workaround we shipped (plugin side)

Schedule deferred work from a queue whose processor was created at **register time** (outside any hook context), so the async context carries no released root work; add drain-aware bounded retry. Verified in production: the same `/new` then completes `sessions.create` in 62 ms while the background embedded run finishes successfully (~37 s, real output).

## Suggested fix

At the plugin-facing `runEmbeddedAgent` boundary, establish an independent/detached root-work admission (or otherwise clear/re-root the inherited ALS context) before submitting work, so deferred plugin tasks are not judged by the caller's released context. The existing `runWithGatewayIndependentRootWorkAdmission` / `runWithGatewayDetachedWorkAdmission` helpers look purpose-built for exactly this.

Happy to open a PR along these lines if the direction sounds right.

## Severity Assessment

### CVSS Assessment

| Metric | v3.1 | v4.0 |
|--------|------|------|
| **Score** | 7.6 / 10.0 | 8.6 / 10.0 |
| **Severity** | High | High |
| **Vector** | CVSS:3.1/AV:A/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:L | CVSS:4.0/AV:A/AC:L/AT:N/PR:N/UI:P/VC:H/VI:H/VA:L/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N |
| **Calculator** | [CVSS v3.1 Calculator](https://www.first.org/cvss/calculator/3.1#CVSS:3.1/AV:A/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:L) | [CVSS v4.0 Calculator](https://www.first.org/cvss/calculator/4.0#CVSS:4.0/AV:A/AC:L/AT:N/PR:N/UI:P/VC:H/VI:H/VA:L/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N) |

### Threat Model Alignment
**Classification:** `security-specific`

OpenClaw’s trust model treats Mattermost slash callbacks as a protected webhook surface: the docs require an explicit HTTPS `callbackUrl`, state that non-HTTPS derived callbacks must fail closed, and authenticate callback requests with Mattermost-issued command tokens on a `plugin` route. A cleartext fallback callback therefore is not an intended public capability or harmless operator convenience; it weakens the transport and token-auth boundary that guards command execution. That makes the finding security-specific rather than a generic hardening request.

## Impact
When Mattermost native slash commands are enabled without an explicit HTTPS callback URL, OpenClaw auto-registers a plain-HTTP callback and then treats any captured slash-command token as valid for every registered command. An on-path attacker can replay that token to inject arbitrary `/oc_*` or native skill commands as a spoofed Mattermost sender.

## Affected Component
**Files:** `extensions/mattermost/src/mattermost/monitor-slash.ts:151-180`, `extensions/mattermost/src/mattermost/slash-commands.ts:540-572`, `extensions/mattermost/src/mattermost/slash-state.ts:94-117`, `extensions/mattermost/src/mattermost/slash-http.ts:243-255`
```ts
// slash-commands.ts
export function resolveCallbackUrl(params: {...}): string {
  if (params.config.callbackUrl) {
    return params.config.callbackUrl;
  }
  ...
  return `http://${host}:${params.gatewayPort}${path}`;
}

// monitor-slash.ts
const slashCallbackUrl = resolveCallbackUrl({
  config: slashConfig,
  gatewayPort: slashGatewayPort,
  gatewayHost: params.cfg.gateway?.customBindHost ?? undefined,
});

// slash-state.ts
const tokenSet = new Set(commandTokens);
const handler = createSlashCommandHttpHandler({ ..., commandTokens: tokenSet, ... });

// slash-http.ts
if (commandTokens.size === 0 || !commandTokens.has(payload.token)) {
  sendJsonResponse(res, 401, { text: "Unauthorized: invalid command token." });
  return;
}
const trigger = payload.command.replace(/^\//, "").trim();
const commandText = resolveCommandText(trigger, payload.text, triggerMap);
```

## Technical Reproduction
1. Configure Mattermost native slash commands, set `gateway.customBindHost` to a host that the Mattermost server can reach, and omit `channels.mattermost.commands.callbackUrl`.
2. Start OpenClaw. `registerMattermostMonitorSlashCommands()` derives and registers a callback like `http://gateway.example.com:18789/api/channels/mattermost/command`.
3. Invoke any legitimate slash command once from Mattermost while observing the callback traffic on the network path between Mattermost and OpenClaw.
4. Capture the POST body token, then replay a forged request directly to `/api/channels/mattermost/command`, for example:
   ```text
   token=<captured-token>&team_id=T1&channel_id=C1&user_id=<allowlisted-user>&user_name=owner&command=/oc_model&text=gpt-5.4
   ```
5. Observe that `createSlashCommandHttpHandler()` accepts the request because it only checks whether `payload.token` is in the per-account token set, then executes the attacker-controlled `command`, `text`, `channel_id`, and `user_id` values.

## Demonstrated Impact
`resolveCallbackUrl()` emits `http://...` whenever operators rely on the default derived callback, so the reusable Mattermost command token is transported in cleartext. `activateSlashCommands()` then discards the trigger-to-token mapping and keeps only a `Set<string>` of valid tokens, and `createSlashCommandHttpHandler()` authorizes any callback whose token is present in that set. Because the callback route is intentionally registered as `auth: "plugin"`, the attacker does not need gateway bearer auth once a single token is observed. A passive or active network attacker can therefore capture one slash-command callback, then forge arbitrary `/oc_*` or native skill command payloads under any sender/channel values that satisfy Mattermost channel policy.

## Environment
Tested against `87eeab703465eb42481946b130b9122c3e0fb587` on current `main`, with the bundled Mattermost plugin, `channels.mattermost.commands.native=true`, a reachable non-loopback `gateway.customBindHost`, and no explicit `channels.mattermost.commands.callbackUrl` override.

## Remediation Advice
Fail closed when the derived Mattermost slash-command callback is not HTTPS, and preserve per-command token bindings so each callback token only authorizes the trigger that Mattermost issued it for.

Version: 2026.10.10; the function is the same on 2026.11.4 and on main (68a839b93). Follow-up to #171351: #171371 fixed that issue's post-ready admission path in `combined-store-gateway.ts`, but `isConfiguredAgentDatabaseTarget` itself is unchanged, and the boot database preflight still calls it once per row. (#170612 made the deletion and discovery matchers linear; this check was not part of it.)

**What happens**

`isConfiguredAgentDatabaseTarget(targets, agentId, pathname)` is one `targets.some(...)`. For every target whose `path` is not `pathname`, it calls `fs.statSync(target.path)`, `fs.statSync(pathname)`, and when both exist `isSameOpenClawAgentDatabasePath` (realpath both, stat both). Nothing is cached between calls.

The database preflight calls it once per candidate row, with the configured `{agentId, path}` targets followed by every candidate path as `{path}` (no agentId). A target with no agentId matches any owner, so every row walks every earlier target. The startup session migration calls it once per registered agent database too. With N agents that is N rows times about 2N targets, up to six sync file calls per miss, all on the main thread before the gateway is ready.

**Measured**

- About 470 agents, prod-shaped state: 8.8M main-thread file calls during boot, 7.1M of them under this function.
- Local, 471 configured agents with 411 databases, two stock and two patched boots alternating: main-thread file calls before ready 4.24M and 4.27M (4.06M and 4.10M in this function) to 180K and 174K (0); the `cli.bootstrap.admission.database-readiness` phase 23.1 s and 10.4 s to 1.1 s and 0.7 s; the `ready` line 101.6 s and 36.4 s to 42.4 s and 14.0 s.
- A 400-row x 400-target synthetic call: 119,400 file calls before, 0 after the change below.

**Suggested fix** (same answer for every row)

```js
function isConfiguredAgentDatabaseTarget(targets, agentId, pathname) {
	const owner = (target) => agentId === void 0 || target.agentId === void 0 || target.agentId === agentId;
	if (targets.some((target) => owner(target) && target.path === pathname)) return true;
	let exists;
	for (const target of targets) {
		if (!owner(target) || !fs.statSync(target.path, { throwIfNoEntry: false })) continue;
		exists ??= !!fs.statSync(pathname, { throwIfNoEntry: false });
		if (!exists) return false;
		if (isSameOpenClawAgentDatabasePath(target.path, pathname)) return true;
	}
	return false;
}
```

An exact owner path answers with a string compare; the row is stat'd once; a missing row stops at the first existing target. This removes the file calls, not the loop: rows still string-compare against every target, and a row with no exact target still takes the file walk. The only behaviour difference is in error cases: a stat or realpath error that the stock loop would hit on an earlier target is not hit when an exact match answers first. We checked it against the stock function on 4,000 random rows (missing files, symlinks, hard links, a symlink loop, ENOTDIR paths): same answers everywhere. A further step would be one `createOpenClawAgentDatabasePathMatcher().createIndex()` per preflight instead of a per-row walk.

**A second quadratic, fresh state only**

When the agent directories do not exist yet, the missing-suffix alias probe runs for every pair of agents, so a first boot with many agents also pays N^2 probes.

