## What Problem This Solves

Fixes browser notifications using a linked local profile’s permissions instead of the browser’s current OpenClaw ID membership permissions.

## User Impact

Notifications follow the current ID role and session visibility, including role changes and removal. A separate locally paired browser keeps its local role. Signing in locally in the same browser retains its ID device binding; if that binding is unavailable during subscription, reconnect before enabling notifications again.

## Why This Change Was Made

Resolve notification recipients through the existing durable device binding and membership authority, then pass the current ID principal through the existing session policy. Subscription registration checks the matching binding before admission and persistence. No subscription schema or protocol changes.

## Evidence

- The committed ID-policy regression has seven expected recipient-selection failures on base `a8a7ce94eb544f3c39cf25f674471c3f1e3fe4ab`; the permitted-member control passes. All eight cases pass with the repair.
- Seven companion test files passed. The final ID-policy/approval run passed 26/26 tests. Cold worker preparation exceeded the isolated VM's default 120-second test budget; the final targeted run used `--testTimeout=600000`, without changing repository timeouts or assertions.
- Core typecheck, all three affected test typecheck shards, targeted lint, format, and `git diff --check` pass.
- Local autoreview of the complete change against the base above, reviewed head `0abe160865adf32b515b4abb0da4c4acab881472`: P0-P2, no actionable findings. Review covered the supplied patch and source context; the reviewer did not execute tests. Local ClawSweeper was unavailable; hosted ClawSweeper identified the same mock-annotation placement defect as CI. The follow-up moves the isolation marker immediately above its factory; all runtime and test statements are unchanged. The fresh hosted review confirmed the fix and found no further actionable code defects; this excerpt addresses its request for inspectable replay output.
