issues.body:
Found while auditing the host this project builds against. Every cited line was opened and every quote checked against HEAD before filing.

**Location.** `src/main.rs:6342`, `:6400-6401`, `:6476-6487`; `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/security/estop.rs:51-56`, `:121-146`. Documented guarantee at `docs/book/src/security/model.md:69`.

**Code.**

```rust
// main.rs, inside fn handle_estop_command
let mut manager = security::EstopManager::load(&config.security.estop, config_dir)?;
manager.engage(engage_level)?;
println!("{}", t("cli-estop-engaged", "Estop engaged."));

// estop.rs
pub fn is_engaged(&self) -> bool {
    self.kill_all || self.network_kill
        || !self.blocked_domains.is_empty() || !self.frozen_tools.is_empty()
}
```

The documentation states: "**Emergency stop**: zeroclaw estop halts all in-flight tool calls. With `[security.estop] enabled = true`, resuming requires an OTP."

**Failure path.** An operator watching the agent misbehave runs `zeroclaw estop`, or `--tool shell`, `--network`, `--domain x.com`. The CLI writes `estop-state.json`, prints "Estop engaged.", and a status block reporting `kill_all: active`. The running agent keeps executing tool calls, and the next `zeroclaw agent` run is equally unaffected. `EstopManager::load` appears seven times in the tree: six inside `estop.rs`'s own `#[cfg(test)]` block and once at `main.rs:6342`. `is_engaged()` has exactly one caller, the status printer. `network_kill`, `blocked_domains`, and `frozen_tools` have zero enforcement consumers. `EstopManager` exposes only `load`, `state_path`, `status`, `engage`, and `resume`; there is no `is_tool_frozen` or `is_domain_blocked` to call. There is no file watcher anywhere in `src` or `zeroclaw-runtime/src`, no IPC, and no signal handling, so a separate CLI process has no channel to the running agent even in principle. The only real halt is SIGTERM, which `docs/book/src/getting-started/yolo.md:35` lists as the degraded YOLO behavior, meaning the documented guarantee holds in no mode. The one enforcing estop in the tree is `robot-kit`'s unrelated GPIO `estop_active`, which does not gate agent tool calls.

**Fix.** Either implement enforcement (a shared watched state file or a control socket that the tool dispatcher consults before each call, plus a mid-call abort for in-flight work) or remove the command and correct `model.md:69`. Shipping a kill switch that prints success and does nothing is worse than shipping none, because it is reached for during an incident.

---

Happy to open a PR if you want one; say which direction you prefer and I will follow the shape you name rather than guessing.

